  "body": "[The spec says](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-elements.html#attr-output-for):\r\n\r\n> The `for` attribute, if specified, must contain a string consisting of an unordered set of unique space-separated tokens that are case-sensitive, each of which must have the value of an `ID` of an element in the same tree.\r\n\r\nThis seems to suggest that this is valid:\r\n\r\n    <label for=\"name email\">\r\n\r\nHowever, if I try to validate a page with this code (and with matching IDs in form elements), I get two separate errors:\r\n\r\n> Error: Bad value `name email` for attribute `for` on element `label`: An ID must not contain whitespace.\r\n\r\n> Error: The `for` attribute of the `label` element must refer to a non-hidden form control.\r\n\r\nAlso, doing this seems to disable the ability to click the label to focus the matching form control, as shown in the following demo:\r\n\r\nhttps://jsbin.com/kekifaq/edit?html,output\r\n\r\nSo I guess my questions are:\r\n\r\n* Is it valid to use multiple values for the `for` attribute?\r\n* If so, what are some examples that demonstrate how this would be used?\r\n* And why does this seem to break focus functionality and validation?\r\n\r\nJust wondering because I came across this in the spec but couldn't figure out how it's useful.",
